4	- `minimize_1an_r3.py`: its docstring is the frozen specification;
5	- `RESULT-r3.json`;
6	- `OBSERVATIONS.json`: the exact data per variant;
7	- `PRIOR-REVIEW-r2.md`: the review that motivated r3;
8	- `RESULT-1AM-SUPERSEDE.json`: an earlier separate SUPERSEDE analysis;
9	- `SCHEMA.md`.
10	
11	Use only these files. You cannot run code; recompute by hand.
12	
13	Check each of the following:
14	1. **Spec fidelity.** Does the code implement the r3 docstring?
15	   - separating semantics D(p,q);
16	   - hitting sets;
17	   - the classes;
18	   - identity-lookup flags;
19	   - forced cluster pairs;
20	   - the operation test;
21	   - the coherent C2.
22	
23	   Does r3 actually fix each prior-review item it claims to fix (D1, D2, D3, D4/D5, D7)? Which prior items remain open?
24	2. **Recomputation.** For every operation and variant, recompute by hand from OBSERVATIONS.json:
25	   - D(p,q) for each opposite-outcome pair;
26	   - the unseparated pairs;
27	   - the minimum and inclusion-minimal hitting sets;
28	   - the FORMAL-REQUIRED variables.
29	
30	   Report mismatches.
31	3. **Instrument weaknesses.** In particular:
32	   - (a) Is the identity-lookup flag informative when an operation has only 2 legality events? Propose a better criterion.
